cache: node mask 0 and 1 are the same device everywhere except in the cache key.

`0002` made a PSO rejection say what mismatched. This is what it found, and the
answer was in the arithmetic rather than in any one line of the log.

Every rejection in a Requiem session looked like this, and there were about fifty
per launch, permanently:

    d3d12_cached_pipeline_state_validate: PSO compatibility hash mismatch:
      expected 473303cf86190c27, blob has 473302cf86190a74, shaders identical
    d3d12_pipeline_state_create: Rejected blob was validated against 1 RTV(s),
      DSV 0, 1x0 samples, topology 3, flags 0

`shaders identical`, so it is not the shaders. And in *every* observed case the
two hashes differed by exactly `0x100000001b3` — the FNV-1 64-bit prime.

THAT DIFFERENCE NAMES THE FIELD, which is the part worth keeping. The hash is
`h = (h * P) ^ v` iterated over the descriptor, and the last two inputs are
`node_mask` then `flags`. The rejection line reports `flags 0`, so the final XOR
contributes nothing and the last step is a pure multiply by P — which makes a
whole-prime difference mean the *penultimate* input differed by one in bit 0.
Nothing else can produce that signature: a `flags` differing by one gives a
difference of one, and any earlier field is multiplied by P again on the way out
and emerges unrecognisable. So it is `node_mask`, and it is 0 against 1.

**vkd3d already agrees those are the same device, in its own code.**
`debug_ignored_node_mask` only complains when `mask && mask != 1`, so zero and
one are indistinguishable to it; and every entry point that takes a NodeMask
discards it after that check — `CreateRootSignature`, `GetCustomHeapProperties`,
`GetResourceAllocationInfo3`, `CreateCommandList`. Pipeline creation never reads
it at all; `state.c` only copies it out of the D3D12 descriptor. D3D12 itself
treats 0 and 1 as equivalent on a single-adapter device.

Then the raw value went into the cache compatibility key. An engine that passes 0
down one path and 1 down another — both correct, both equivalent — writes a blob
under one and is refused under the other. The same pair matches on Windows.

**This is not a stale cache and not an unstable hash**, and both were checked
before this patch was written. A blob whose build differs is rejected earlier, by
`vkd3d_build`, with a different error (`D3D12_ERROR_DRIVER_VERSION_MISMATCH`, not
`E_INVALIDARG`) — so anything that reaches this comparison was written by this
binary on this GPU. `VKD3D_REVISION` is Vessel's packaging number and is not part
of `vkd3d_build`, so bumping it cannot cause this either. And the hash is a pure
function of the descriptor.

**Normalised rather than removed.** Dropping the field would be defensible, since
nothing acts on it — but it would change the hash of every existing blob and
invalidate caches that are currently valid. Mapping 0 to 1 changes only the
descriptors that were being refused wrongly, and a mask of 1 hashes exactly as it
did before.

**What it costs, stated at its real size.** A refused blob returns `E_INVALIDARG`
and the engine recompiles that pipeline, so this is compile time and not
correctness. `docs/TODO.md` records 45 mismatches in one run and 50 in the next —
roughly 1.5% of 3,025 shaders, not all of them. The log reads far worse than that
because each event prints four to six lines: the mismatch, the summary, and one
`RTV n format` line per render target. Fifty events is about 250 lines.

Answers the *Done when* on `docs/TODO.md` #42, which asked for the mismatching
field to be named.

